(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-pulmonary-quiz-version-4"]={"title":"Pulmonary Physiology — Quiz 3 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Compared with the systemic circulation, the pulmonary circulation carries the same total blood flow (about 5 L/min) but with a much smaller pressure drop across it (about 12 mmHg) because pulmonary vascular resistance is approximately...","choices":["1/7 that of the systemic circulation","essentially zero, offering no resistance at all","identical to that of the systemic circulation","7 times that of the systemic circulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary vascular resistance is roughly one-seventh that of the systemic circulation, which is why the same cardiac output (about 5 L/min) can flow through the lungs with a much smaller pressure drop (about 12 mmHg) than across the systemic circuit. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 39"},{"q":"Normal pulmonary artery pressures are considerably lower than systemic arterial pressures. Which set of values correctly approximates normal pulmonary artery systolic, diastolic, and mean pressures?","choices":["Systolic 8 mmHg, diastolic 25 mmHg, mean 15 mmHg","Systolic 120 mmHg, diastolic 80 mmHg, mean 93 mmHg","Systolic 25 mmHg, diastolic 8 mmHg, mean 15 mmHg","Systolic 60 mmHg, diastolic 40 mmHg, mean 50 mmHg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Normal pulmonary artery pressure is approximately 25/8 mmHg (systolic/diastolic), with a mean around 15 mmHg — far lower than typical systemic arterial pressure, consistent with the pulmonary circuit's much lower vascular resistance. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 39"},{"q":"Within the pulmonary circulation, an increase in vascular pressure characteristically...","choices":["increases pulmonary vascular resistance","has no effect on pulmonary vascular resistance","only affects resistance in the systemic circulation, not the pulmonary circulation","decreases pulmonary vascular resistance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Unlike some other vascular beds, increasing pressure within the pulmonary circulation decreases pulmonary vascular resistance, largely because higher pressure recruits previously unperfused capillaries and distends already-open vessels, both of which lower overall resistance. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 41"},{"q":"Two related mechanisms — recruitment (opening previously closed capillaries) and distension (widening of already-open vessels) — both act in the pulmonary circulation to...","choices":["increase pulmonary vascular resistance as pressure or flow increases","cause alveolar collapse","only occur in the systemic circulation, not the lungs","decrease pulmonary vascular resistance as pressure or flow increases"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. As pulmonary blood pressure or flow rises (such as during exercise), recruitment of previously unperfused capillaries and distension of already-open vessels both act to decrease overall pulmonary vascular resistance, allowing large increases in flow with only modest increases in pressure. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 42"},{"q":"Alveolar hypoxia (low oxygen within an alveolus) has a distinctive local effect on the pulmonary vasculature: it...","choices":["has no effect on local pulmonary blood flow despite altering systemic vessels","increases blood flow to that lung region, via localized vasodilation","produces vasoconstriction only in the bronchial circulation, not the pulmonary vessels","decreases blood flow to that lung region, via localized vasoconstriction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Unlike most systemic vascular beds (where local hypoxia causes vasodilation), alveolar hypoxia in the lung triggers localized pulmonary vasoconstriction, decreasing blood flow to that poorly ventilated region — a mechanism that helps redirect blood flow toward better-ventilated alveoli, optimizing ventilation-perfusion matching. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 43"},{"q":"In the upright lung, blood flow distribution is uneven, with the bottom (base) of the lung normally receiving...","choices":["exactly equal perfusion compared with the top (apex), a pattern that holds constant during exercise","more perfusion than the top (apex), even though this same relative distribution persists during exercise","less perfusion than the top (apex), with this pattern becoming more pronounced during exercise","perfusion that reverses to become apex-dominant specifically once exercise begins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Due to the effects of gravity on the low-pressure pulmonary circulation, the base of the upright lung normally receives more blood flow than the apex; exercise increases total pulmonary blood flow, but the same relative base-greater-than-apex distribution pattern is maintained. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 44"},{"q":"Applying Starling forces to the pulmonary capillaries: outward (filtration-favoring) forces include capillary hydrostatic pressure (about 7 mmHg), negative interstitial pressure (about 8 mmHg), and interstitial osmotic pressure (about 14 mmHg), which together are nearly, but not completely, balanced by the main inward force of...","choices":["pulmonary arterial pressure, about 15 mmHg","central venous pressure, about 2 mmHg","alveolar gas pressure, about 0 mmHg (atmospheric)","plasma (colloid) osmotic pressure, about 28 mmHg"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Plasma osmotic pressure (about 28 mmHg) is the dominant inward (fluid-retaining) force, nearly balancing the roughly 29 mmHg of combined outward forces, leaving a small net filtration pressure of about +1 mmHg that drives a slow, continuous, and normally well-drained outward fluid movement. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 48"},{"q":"Because the net Starling filtration pressure across pulmonary capillaries is slightly positive (about +1 mmHg), fluid continuously (but slowly) filters out of the capillaries. Under normal conditions, this fluid is prevented from accumulating and flooding the alveoli mainly because...","choices":["the alveolar epithelium actively pumps fluid back into the capillaries against the gradient","surfactant chemically binds and absorbs the excess interstitial fluid as it accumulates","the lymphatic system continuously drains it, maintaining a negative pleural/interstitial pressure","filtration is actually zero under normal conditions, so no drainage is needed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pulmonary lymphatic system continuously drains the small, ongoing capillary filtrate, maintaining a negative interstitial/pleural pressure that keeps the alveoli dry and helps hold the lung expanded against the chest wall — fluid only accumulates pathologically when production outpaces this drainage. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 50"},{"q":"A thin layer of mucoid fluid lies between the parietal and visceral pleura. This layer's main functional purpose is to...","choices":["digest inhaled particulate matter that becomes trapped within the pleural space itself","actively transport oxygen directly between the lung surface and the chest wall tissue","reduce friction between the lung and chest wall as they slide against each other during ventilation","generate the negative intrapleural pressure by itself, independent of the lymphatics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The thin mucoid fluid film between the parietal and visceral pleural layers lubricates the interface, minimizing friction as the lung surface slides against the chest wall with each breath. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 50"},{"q":"Alveoli composing the respiratory unit have walls specialized for gas exchange, with an average alveolar diameter of approximately...","choices":["0.02 mm","0.2 mm","20 mm","2 mm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The roughly 300 million alveoli in the two lungs average about 0.2 mm in diameter, a small size that (together with their thin walls) maximizes the total surface area available for efficient gas exchange. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 57"},{"q":"In blood-gas terminology, the partial pressure of a gas (such as O2) specifically represents...","choices":["the driving force for diffusion, determined by that gas's percentage in the mixture","the percentage of hemoglobin binding sites occupied by that gas","a fixed value that stays constant no matter how the gas mixture's composition changes","the absolute quantity of gas dissolved in a given volume of blood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Partial pressure reflects a specific gas's proportional contribution to total pressure (depending on its percentage in the gas mixture) and serves as the driving force for its diffusion — distinct from 'saturation' (percent of hemoglobin binding sites occupied) or 'content' (absolute quantity of gas carried per volume of blood). — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 66"},{"q":"Unlike hemoglobin 'saturation' (a percentage with no units), oxygen 'content' in blood is expressed as...","choices":["the driving pressure for diffusion, identical to partial pressure","a unitless percentage, identical to saturation","always identical to arterial PO2 regardless of hemoglobin level","an absolute quantity, such as mL of O2 per unit volume of blood"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. 'Content' refers to the absolute quantity of oxygen actually carried in a given volume of blood (e.g., mL O2 per 100 mL blood), in contrast to 'saturation,' which is simply the percentage of hemoglobin binding sites occupied by oxygen, independent of how much hemoglobin is actually present. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 66"},{"q":"A ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) ratio of zero at a given lung region specifically describes a situation in which that region has...","choices":["neither ventilation nor perfusion at all","perfectly matched ventilation and perfusion","ventilation but no blood flow (dead space)","blood flow but no ventilation (a shunt-like state)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A V/Q ratio of zero describes a region receiving blood flow (perfusion) but no ventilation at all — functioning like a right-to-left shunt, since blood passing through cannot pick up oxygen or release CO2 there. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 64"},{"q":"A ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) ratio approaching infinity at a given lung region specifically describes a region with...","choices":["blood flow but no ventilation, functioning as a shunt-like state","matched ventilation and perfusion, near a V/Q ratio of about 0.8","ventilation but no blood flow (physiological dead space)","perfectly matched ventilation and perfusion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A very high (approaching infinite) V/Q ratio describes a region that is being ventilated but receives no blood flow, functioning as physiological dead space — air reaches the alveolus, but there is no blood present to exchange gas with. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 64"},{"q":"Pleural effusion (excess fluid accumulation in the pleural space) can result when the normal balance between pulmonary capillary fluid filtration and lymphatic drainage is disrupted — specifically when...","choices":["alveolar ventilation increases beyond metabolic demand","fluid production exceeds the lymphatic system's drainage capacity","the lymphatic system's drainage capacity always exceeds fluid production, regardless of any other factor","surfactant production increases beyond normal levels"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Because pulmonary capillaries normally filter a small, continuous amount of fluid that is cleared just as continuously by the lymphatic system, pleural effusion develops whenever fluid production comes to exceed the lymphatics' capacity to drain it — whether from increased capillary permeability, reduced plasma osmotic pressure, elevated capillary pressure, or lymphatic obstruction. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 50"},{"q":"Diffusing capacity for oxygen (a measure of how readily O2 crosses the respiratory membrane) characteristically rises during exercise mainly because...","choices":["diffusing capacity actually falls during exercise, opposite to the true relationship","pulmonary capillary recruitment and distension increase the surface area available for gas exchange","the respiratory membrane becomes measurably thinner as blood flow accelerates","alveolar ventilation falls even as pulmonary blood flow rises during exertion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. During exercise, increased pulmonary blood flow triggers recruitment of previously closed capillaries and distension of already-open ones, expanding the total surface area (and blood volume) available for gas exchange, which raises measured diffusing capacity above its resting value. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 63"},{"q":"The overall work of breathing includes both elastic work (stretching lung tissue and overcoming surface tension) and resistive (frictional) work. Resistive work specifically refers to the energy needed to...","choices":["synthesize fresh surfactant with each and every single breath taken","overcome airway and tissue friction as air and lung structures move","stretch lung tissue and overcome alveolar surface tension","actively pump blood through the pulmonary circulation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Resistive (frictional) work of breathing is the energy required to overcome airway resistance to airflow and the friction of lung/chest wall tissues moving against each other, distinct from elastic work, which is the energy needed to stretch the lung's elastic tissue and overcome alveolar surface tension. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 15"},{"q":"A normal, well-matched ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) ratio at rest is approximately...","choices":["0.8","0.08","8.0","Exactly 1.0, a perfect match at all times"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The overall resting ventilation-perfusion ratio for the lungs as a whole is approximately 0.8 (roughly 4 L/min alveolar ventilation to 5 L/min pulmonary blood flow), reflecting a well-matched but not perfectly 1:1 balance between ventilation and perfusion. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 65"},{"q":"During quiet breathing, the diaphragm is the principal muscle of inspiration; during forced (deep) inspiration, additional accessory muscles are recruited, including the...","choices":["external intercostal and accessory neck muscles (e.g., sternocleidomastoid, scalenes)","no muscles beyond the diaphragm, even during maximal forced inspiration","smooth muscle within the bronchiolar walls that widens the airway lumen","internal intercostal and abdominal wall muscles used for forced expiration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beyond the diaphragm, forced/deep inspiration recruits accessory muscles including the external intercostals (which help elevate the rib cage) and neck muscles such as the sternocleidomastoid and scalenes, which further expand the thoracic cavity. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 10"},{"q":"During forced (active) expiration, such as during heavy exercise, the muscles primarily responsible for driving air out more forcefully than during quiet expiration are the...","choices":["external intercostal and diaphragm muscles","only bronchiolar smooth muscle","no additional muscles beyond quiet expiration's passive recoil","internal intercostal and abdominal muscles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. While quiet expiration is largely passive (driven by elastic recoil), forced expiration actively recruits the internal intercostal muscles (pulling the rib cage down/in) and the abdominal muscles (pushing the diaphragm upward), together generating a more forceful, rapid exhalation. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 12"},{"q":"Gas exchange across the respiratory membrane occurs specifically at the level of the...","choices":["alveolar sacs","tracheal wall","main bronchi","laryngeal folds"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Actual gas exchange between air and blood occurs at the level of the alveolar sacs and their surrounding pulmonary capillaries, the terminal structures of the respiratory unit specialized for diffusion given their thin walls and large combined surface area. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 57"},{"q":"Compared with pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure (about 7 mmHg), central systemic capillary hydrostatic pressures are generally higher, which helps explain why the pulmonary circulation is especially well suited to...","choices":["generate very high systemic-level blood pressure within the pulmonary vasculature itself","eliminate any ongoing need for lymphatic drainage within the lung tissue","maintain relatively dry alveoli, favoring efficient gas exchange rather than fluid leakage","promote large-scale, continuous fluid leakage into the alveoli under normal conditions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pulmonary circulation's comparatively low hydrostatic pressure (about 7 mmHg) keeps net Starling forces only slightly favoring filtration, helping keep the thin alveolar-capillary interface relatively dry — essential for efficient gas exchange, since even modest fluid accumulation would impair diffusion. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 48"},{"q":"The medullary rhythmicity area, one of the three classic components of brainstem respiratory control, is primarily responsible for...","choices":["controlling only voluntary breath-holding, with no automatic rhythm role","sensing arterial oxygen levels directly via peripheral chemoreceptor input","generating the basic, automatic rhythm of inspiration and expiration","regulating bronchiolar smooth muscle tone exclusively"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The medullary rhythmicity area contains the neurons that generate the basic automatic rhythm of breathing (alternating inspiratory and expiratory phases), which the pneumotaxic and apneustic centers then modulate. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 88"},{"q":"Peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid and aortic bodies, unlike central chemoreceptors, are especially important for detecting...","choices":["a substantial fall in arterial PO2, in addition to changes in PCO2 and pH","blood glucose concentration exclusively, independent of any gas tension","core body temperature exclusively, rather than any blood gas value","changes in circulating blood volume exclusively, not arterial gas tensions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. While central chemoreceptors dominate the CO2/pH-driven minute-to-minute regulation of ventilation, peripheral chemoreceptors are especially important for detecting a substantial drop in arterial PO2 (as well as responding to PCO2 and pH), providing a critical backup oxygen-sensing drive to breathe. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 94"},{"q":"The Hering-Breuer inflation reflex helps protect the lungs from overinflation by...","choices":["using stretch receptors in the lung/airways to inhibit further inspiration once the lungs are sufficiently inflated","using peripheral chemoreceptors positioned in the carotid and aortic bodies to detect a fall in PO2 during lung inflation","stimulating further inspiration the more the lungs continue to inflate during a breath","having no functional role in normal quiet breathing or protection against overinflation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. As the lungs inflate during a large breath, stretch receptors in the airway walls send inhibitory signals (via the vagus nerve) to the inspiratory center, terminating inspiration before overinflation occurs — a protective feedback loop known as the Hering-Breuer inflation reflex. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 88"},{"q":"If air enters the pleural space (a pneumothorax), abolishing the normally negative intrapleural pressure, the affected lung tends to...","choices":["expand further beyond its normal resting volume","collapse, due to its own unopposed elastic recoil","remain completely unaffected in size","immediately become stiffer without any change in volume"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lung's natural elastic recoil constantly pulls it inward, normally opposed by the negative intrapleural pressure holding it against the chest wall; if air enters the pleural space and abolishes this negative pressure, the lung is no longer held expanded and collapses under its own unopposed elastic recoil. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 17"},{"q":"Pulmonary capillaries, in cross section, are notable for being able to accommodate red blood cells despite being...","choices":["similar in diameter to the aorta, the body's largest blood vessel","extremely narrow, often barely wider than a single red blood cell","completely impermeable to the passage of any red blood cells at all","distinctly wider in diameter than the pulmonary artery itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary capillaries are so narrow that red blood cells often must traverse them essentially single-file, deforming slightly as needed — a tight fit that maximizes contact time and proximity between blood and the thin respiratory membrane for efficient gas exchange. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 58"},{"q":"Arterial H+ concentration influences the medullary inspiratory area, but because H+ crosses the blood-brain barrier poorly, its most important direct ventilatory effect is instead exerted through...","choices":["direct diffusion of H+ across the blood-brain barrier into the CSF","the peripheral chemoreceptors, which are directly exposed to arterial blood","the pneumotaxic center exclusively, bypassing chemoreceptors entirely","skeletal muscle chemoreceptors located within actively contracting muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Since the blood-brain barrier limits direct H+ access to central chemoreceptors, arterial H+ instead exerts its most direct, immediate ventilatory stimulus through the peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid/aortic bodies), which are directly bathed in arterial blood and can sense H+ (and PO2) changes right away. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 91"},{"q":"Pulmonary vascular resistance being inversely related to pressure (falling as pressure rises) stands in contrast to the systemic circulation, where structures like arterioles use active smooth muscle constriction/dilation as the dominant resistance-control mechanism. The pulmonary pattern instead depends mainly on...","choices":["a completely rigid, non-distensible vessel wall structure","passive mechanical recruitment and distension of vessels rather than active smooth muscle tone changes","active smooth muscle constriction as the dominant and only mechanism, identical to systemic arterioles","renal regulation of blood volume"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rather than relying primarily on active smooth muscle constriction/dilation (as systemic arterioles do), the pulmonary circulation's pressure-resistance relationship is explained largely by passive mechanical recruitment (opening previously closed vessels) and distension (widening already-open vessels) as pressure rises. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 42"},{"q":"Functional residual capacity (FRC), the volume of air remaining in the lungs after a normal, quiet expiration, is the sum of...","choices":["tidal volume and inspiratory reserve volume","expiratory reserve volume and residual volume","only tidal volume, without any reserve or residual component","vital capacity combined with the normal tidal volume value"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Functional residual capacity equals the expiratory reserve volume (the air that could still be forcibly exhaled) plus the residual volume (the air that can never be exhaled), representing the lung's resting volume between normal breaths. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 27"}]};
